{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Conditioned pain modulation (CPM) is considered a human proxy for descending inhibitory pain pathways. However, there is wide variation in the CPM response described in the literature and ongoing debate about its utility. METHODS: Here we explored CPM in women with (n = 59) and without (n = 26) chronic pelvic pain (CPP), aiming to determine the magnitude of effect and factors influencing variability in the CPM response. RESULTS: Using a pressure pain threshold test stimulus and ischaemic pressure cuff conditioning stimulus (CS), we found no significant difference in the mean CPM effect between CPP and control participants. Using a robust statistical method (+/-2 standard error of measurement) to further investigate CPM, there was no significant difference in the proportion exhibiting inhibition between controls and CPP participants (X2  = 0.003, p = 0.96). Notably, only 23.1% of our healthy controls demonstrated a \"true\" CPM effect (n = 4 inhibitory, n = 2 facilitatory). Despite a rich data set, we were unable to identify any single questionnaire, clinical or psychophysical covariate correlating with the CPM effect. CONCLUSIONS: Despite using one of the recommended CPM paradigms we were only able to demonstrate \"true\" CPM in 23.1% of control participants. Thus, the absence of differences between women with and without chronic pelvic pain must be interpreted with caution. Future studies using different CPM paradigms or larger sample sizes may find different results. Although CPM in chronic pain populations is of major theoretical mechanistic interest, the lack of an established assessment standard led us to question its added value in current clinical research.","author_count":31,"contributors":["Demetriou, Lysia","Perro, Danielle","Coxon, Lydia","Krassowski, Michal","Lunde, Claire E","Ferreira-Gomes, Joana","Charrua, Ana","Abreu-Mendes, Pedro","Arendt-Nielsen, Lars","Aziz, Qasim","Birch, Judy","Garbutt, Kurtis","Horne, Andrew","Hoffman, Anja","Hummelshoj, Lone","Meijlink, Jane","Obendorf, Maik","Pogatzki-Zahn, Esther","Sasamoto, Naoko","Terry, Kathryn","Treede, Rolf-Detlef","Vitonis, Allison","Vollert, Jan","Rahmioglu, Nilufer","Becker, Christian M","Cruz, Francisco","Missmer, Stacey A","Zondervan, Krina","Sieberg, Christine B","Nagel, Jens","Vincent, Katy"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-805673"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corpau":["TRiP. P. Consortium"],"country":["Germany","Netherlands","Portugal","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Bayer AG","Boston Children's Hospital","Brigham and Women’s Hospital","Centro Hospitalar Universitário de São João","Endometriosis.org","Harvard University","Heidelberg University","International Painful Bladder Foundation","Massachusetts General Hospital","Merz GmbH & Co. KGaA","Michigan State University","Pelvic Pain Support Network","Queen Mary University of London","University Hospital Münster","University of Edinburgh","University of Exeter","University of Oxford"],"keyword":["Chronic pelvic pain","Conditioned pain modulation","Quantitative sensory testing","Women s health","pain characteristics"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"exploring the value of a well established conditioned pain modulation paradigm in women a translational research in pelvic pain tripp study","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpain.2025.1439563","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["The author(s) declare financial support was received for the research, authorship, and/or publication of this article. This project has received funding from the Innovative Medicines Initiative 2 Joint Undertaking under grant agreement No 777500. This Joint Undertaking receives support from the European Union's Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme and EFPIA Companies. Financial support was provided by the J. Willard and Alice S. Marriott Foundation for establishment of and baseline data collection within the A2A cohort—from which the Boston-based TRiPP population selected for CPM was sampled. This project was pre-registered on clinicaltrials.gov: NCT04001244. Acknowledgements The authors wish to thank Natalie Cuccia for her work on data collection. Center for Neuroplasticity and Pain (CNAP) is supported by the Danish National Research Foundation (DNRF121). The author(s) declare financial support was received for the research, authorship, and/or publication of this article. This project has received funding from the Innovative Medicines Initiative 2 Joint Undertaking under grant agreement No 777500. This Joint Undertaking receives support from the European Union's Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme and EFPIA Companies. Financial support was provided by the J. Willard and Alice S. Marriott Foundation for establishment of and baseline data collection within the A2A cohort—from which the Boston-based TRiPP population selected for CPM was sampled. This project was pre-registered on clinicaltrials.gov: NCT04001244."],"id":"S01-000.00-805673","identifier":[{"id":"PMC11936887","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"40144516","type":"pmid"},{"id":"105002049439","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-f87da777-bf45-4c55-997f-db079ead5b3c"],"keyword":["chronic pelvic pain","conditioned pain modulation","pain characteristics","quantitative sensory testing","women's health"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-30CC5DF439FDC32D2D1552FC46517077"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/file