 Benno I.","rank":44,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"38","corresponding":null,"first":"Christopher N.","key":"kaiserbunburychristophernpau","last":"Kaiser-Bunbury","name":"Kaiser-Bunbury, Christopher N.","rank":45,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Trine","key":"bildetrinepau","last":"Bilde","locid":"96fb8f00-10de-4e6b-a5c0-213ba84ec55d","name":"Bilde, Trine","orcid":"0000-0002-0341-161X","rank":46,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bo","key":"dalsgaardbopau","last":"Dalsgaard","name":"Dalsgaard, Bo","rank":47,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/1365-2435.70068","issn":["02698463","13652435"],"issn_org":["02698463"],"issue":"7","journal":"Functional Ecology","pages":"1678-1692","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell Publishing Ltd","status":"p","title":"Functional Ecology","volume":"39"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105004754978"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-63b85b38-3272-4360-9d76-51d45b90c63f"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"The role of insularity : Plants have few ornithophilous traits but are visited by morphologically more distinct hummingbirds in the Caribbean islands","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-806597","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Canine hip dysplasia (CHD) screening relies on radiographic assessment, but traditional scoring methods often lack consistency due to inter-rater variability. This study presents an AI-driven system for automated measurement of the femoral head center to dorsal acetabular edge (FHC/DAE) distance, a key metric in CHD evaluation. Unlike most AI models that directly classify CHD severity using convolutional neural networks, this system provides an interpretable, measurement-based output to support a more transparent evaluation. The system combines a keypoint regression model for femoral head center localization with a U-Net-based segmentation model for acetabular edge delineation. It was trained on 7967 images for hip joint detection, 571 for keypoints, and 624 for acetabulum segmentation, all from ventrodorsal hip-extended radiographs. On a test set of 70 images, the keypoint model achieved high precision (Euclidean Distance = 0.055 mm; Mean Absolute Error = 0.0034 mm; Mean Squared Error = 2.52 x 10-5 mm2), while the segmentation model showed strong performance (Dice Score = 0.96; Intersection over Union = 0.92). Comparison with expert annotations demonstrated strong agreement (Intraclass Correlation Coefficients = 0.97 and 0.93; Weighted Kappa = 0.86 and 0.79; Standard Error of Measurement = 0.92 to 1.34 mm). By automating anatomical landmark detection, the system enhances standardization, reproducibility, and interpretability in CHD radiographic assessment. Its strong alignment with expert evaluations supports its integration into CHD screening workflows for more objective and efficient diagnosis and CHD scoring.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Franco-Gonçalo, Pedro","Leite, Pedro","Alves-Pimenta, Sofia","Colaço, Bruno","Gonçalves, Lio","Filipe, Vítor","McEvoy, Fintan","Ferreira, Manuel","Ginja, Mário"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-818885"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Portugal"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","INESC TEC","Neadvance Machine Vision","Universidade de Trás-os-Montes e Alto Douro"],"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Canine hip dysplasia","Computer vision","Screening","Semantic segmentation"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a computer aided approach to canine hip dysplasia assessment measuring femoral head acetabulum distance with deep learning","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/app15095087","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-818885","identifier":[{"id":"105004912650","type":"scopus"},{"id":"001485980600001","type":"wos"}],"ids":["ku-3957911f-8774-48c3-afa7-147945d4cd82"],"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Canine hip dysplasia","Computer vision","Regression network","Screening","Semantic segmentation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-16064ED40F8233D23EB79E81FEDF9162"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/app15095087","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/452035995/A_Computer-Aided_Approach_to_Canine_Hip_Dysplasia_Assessment.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"pt","key":"universidade de trás os montes e alto douro","loc":["Portugal","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universidade de Trás-os-Montes e Alto Douro","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"pt","key":"neadvance machine vision","loc":["Portugal","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Neadvance Machine Vision","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"pt","key":"inesc tec","loc":["Portugal","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"INESC TEC","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Veterinary Clinical Sciences, Section for Diagnostic Imaging & Companion Animal Surgery and Clinical Specialties","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Veterinary Clinical Sciences, Section for Veterinary Imaging"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Pedro","key":"francogonçalopau","last":"Franco-Gonçalo","name":"Franco-Gonçalo, Pedro","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Pedro","key":"leitepau","last":"Leite","name":"Leite, Pedro","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Sofia","key":"alvespimentapau","last":"Alves-Pimenta","name":"Alves-Pimenta, Sofia","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Bruno","key":"colaçopau","last":"Colaço","name":"Colaço, Bruno","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","first":"Lio","key":"gonçalvespau","last":"Gonçalves","name":"Gonçalves, Lio","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","first":"Vítor","key":"filipepau","last":"Filipe","name":"Filipe, Vítor","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Fintan","key":"mcevoypau","last":"McEvoy","locid":"38cc3ea7-06d4-43f9-b239-092f41bcd16b","name":"McEvoy, Fintan","orcid":"0000-0003-4195-8816","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Manuel","key":"ferreirapau","last":"Ferreira","name":"Ferreira, Manuel","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Mário","key":"ginjapau","last":"Ginja","name":"Ginja, Mário","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/app15095087","issn":["20763417"],"issue":"9","journal":"Applied Sciences","paperid":"5087","status":"p","title":"Applied Sciences","title_org":"Applied Sciences (Switzerland)","volume":"15"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105004912650"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-3957911f-8774-48c3-afa7-147945d4cd82"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A Computer-Aided Approach to Canine Hip Dysplasia Assessment : Measuring Femoral Head-Acetabulum Distance with Deep Learning","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-818885","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Aim: The urgency for remote, reliable and scalable biodiversity monitoring amidst mounting human pressures on ecosystems has sparked worldwide interest in Passive Acoustic Monitoring (PAM), which can track life underwater and on land. However, we lack a unified methodology to report this sampling effort and a comprehensive overview of PAM coverage to gauge its potential as a global research and monitoring tool. To address this gap, we created the Worldwide Soundscapes project, a collaborative network and growing database comprising metadata from 416 datasets across all realms (terrestrial, marine, freshwater and subterranean). Location: Worldwide, 12,343 sites, all ecosystem types. Time Period: 1991 to present. Major Taxa Studied: All soniferous taxa. Methods: We synthesise sampling coverage across spatial, temporal and ecological scales using metadata describing sampling locations, deployment schedules, focal taxa and audio recording parameters. We explore global trends in biological, anthropogenic and geophysical sounds based on 168 selected recordings from 12 ecosystems across all realms. Results: Terrestrial sampling is spatially denser (46 sites per million square kilometre—Mkm2) than aquatic sampling (0.3 and 1.8 sites/Mkm2 in oceans and fresh water) with only two subterranean datasets. Although diel and lunar cycles are well sampled across realms, only marine datasets (55%) comprehensively sample all seasons. Across the 12 ecosystems selected for exploring global acoustic trends, biological sounds showed contrasting diel patterns across ecosystems, declined with distance from the Equator, and were negatively correlated with anthropogenic sounds. Main Conclusions: PAM can inform macroecological studies as well as global conservation and phenology syntheses, but representation can be improved by expanding terrestrial taxonomic scope, sampling coverage in the high seas and subterranean ecosystems, and spatio-temporal replication in freshwater habitats. Overall, this worldwide PAM network holds promise to support cross-realm biodiversity research and monitoring efforts.","author_count":253,"contributors":["Darras, Kevin F.A.","Rountree, Rodney A.","Van Wilgenburg, Steven L.","Cord, Anna F.","Pitz, Frederik","Chen, Youfang","Dong, Lijun","Rocquencourt, Agnès","Desjonquères, Camille","Diaz, Patrick Mauritz","Lin, Tzu Hao","Turco, Théophile","Emmerson, Louise","Bradfer-Lawrence, Tom","Gasc, Amandine","Marley, Sarah","Salton, Marcus","Schillé, Laura","Wensveen, Paul J.","Wu, Shih Hung","Acero-Murcia, Adriana C.","Acevedo-Charry, Orlando","Adam, Matyáš","Aguzzi, Jacopo","Akoglu, Irmak","Amorim, M. Clara P.","Anders, Mina","André, Michel","Antonelli, Alexandre","Do Nascimento, Leandro Aparecido","Appel, Giulliana","Archer, Stephanie","Astaras, Christos","Atemasov, Andrey","Atkinson, Jamieson","Attia, Joël","Baltag, Emanuel","Barbaro, Luc","Basan, Fritjof","Batist, Carly","Baumgarten, Julio Ernesto","Bayle Sempere, Just T.","Bellisario, Kristen","David, Asaf Ben","Berger-Tal, Oded","Bertucci, Frédéric","Betts, Matthew G.","Bhalla, Iqbal S.","Bicudo, Thiago","Bolgan, Marta","Bombaci, Sara","Bota, Gerard","Boullhesen, Martin","Briers, Robert A.","Buchan, Susannah","Budka, Michal","Burchard, Katie","Buscaino, Giuseppa","Calvente, Alice","Campos-Cerqueira, Marconi","Gonçalves, Maria Isabel Carvalho","Ceraulo, Maria","Cerezo-Araujo, Maite","Cerwén, Gunnar","Chaskda, Adams A.","Chistopolova, Maria","Clark, Christopher W.","Cox, Kieran D.","Cretois, Benjamin","Czarnecki, Chapin","da Silva, Luis P.","da Silva, Wigna","De Clippele, Laurence H.","de la Haye, David","de Oliveira Tissiani, Ana Silvia","de Zwaan, Devin","Degano, M. Eugenia","Deichmann, Jessica","del Rio, Joaquin","Devenish, Christian","Díaz-Delgado, Ricardo","Diniz, Pedro","Oliveira-Júnior, Dorgival Diógenes","Dorigo, Thiago","Dröge, Saskia","Duarte, Marina","Duarte, Adam","Dunleavy, Kerry","Dziak, Robert","Elise, Simon","Enari, Hiroto","Enari, Haruka S.","Erbs, F