{"abstract":"Adequate radiographic positioning on the X-ray table is paramount for canine hip dysplasia (HD) screening. The aims of this study were to evaluate femoral parallelism on normal ventrodorsal hip extended (VDHE) view and the effect of femoral angulation (FA) on Norberg Angle (NA) and Hip Congruency Index (HCI). The femoral parallelism was evaluated comparing the alignment of the long femoral axis with the long body axis in normal VDHE views and the effect of FA on NA and HCI on repeated VDHE views with different levels of FA. The femoral long axis in normal VDHE views showed a ranged of FA from −4.85° to 5.85°, mean ± standard deviation (SD) of −0.06 ± 2.41°, 95% CI [−4.88, 4.76°]. In the paired views, the mean ± SD femur adduction of 3.69 ± 1.96° led to a statistically significant decrease NA, and HCI, and femur abduction of 2.89 ± 2.12 led to a statistically significant increase in NA and HCI (p < 0.05). The FA differences were also significantly correlated with both NA differences (r = 0.83) and HCI differences (r = 0.44) (p < 0.001). This work describes a methodology that allows evaluation of femoral parallelism in VDHE views and the results suggest that femur abduction yielded more desirable NA and HCI values and adduction impaired NA and HCI values. The positive linear association of FA with NA and HCI allows the use of regression equations to create corrections, to reduce the influence of poor femoral parallelism in the HD scoring.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Franco-Gonçalo, Pedro","Alves-Pimenta, Sofia","Gonçalves, Lio","Colaço, Bruno","Leite, Pedro","Ribeiro, Alexandrine","Ferreira, Manuel","McEvoy, Fintan","Ginja, Mário"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-679349"],"filters":{"added":"2023-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Portugal"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Neadvance Machine Vision SA","Universidade de Trás-os-Montes e Alto Douro"],"keyword":["Dog","Hip dysplasia"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"femoral parallelism evaluation and impact of variation on canine hip dysplasia assessment","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fvets.2023.1160200","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-679349","identifier":[{"id":"85159923783","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC10196174","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"37215470","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-e712921a-d753-49b6-8bef-95587e27fef9"],"keyword":["Norberg angle","dog","femoral parallelism","hip congruency index","hip dysplasia"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-8692E14D48BA4FDD4F41DD87F79F71E5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fvets.2023.1160200","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/351237413/fvets_10_1160200.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"pt","key":"universidade de trás os montes e alto douro","loc":["Portugal","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universidade de Trás-os-Montes e Alto Douro","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"pt","key":"neadvance machine vision sa","loc":["Portugal","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Neadvance Machine Vision SA","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Veterinary Clinical Sciences, Section for Veterinary Imaging"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Pedro","key":"francogonçalopau","last":"Franco-Gonçalo","name":"Franco-Gonçalo, Pedro","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Sofia","key":"alvespimentapau","last":"Alves-Pimenta","name":"Alves-Pimenta, Sofia","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lio","key":"gonçalvespau","last":"Gonçalves","name":"Gonçalves, Lio","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Bruno","key":"colaçopau","last":"Colaço","name":"Colaço, Bruno","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Pedro","key":"leitepau","last":"Leite","name":"Leite, Pedro","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Alexandrine","key":"ribeiropau","last":"Ribeiro","name":"Ribeiro, Alexandrine","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Manuel","key":"ferreirapau","last":"Ferreira","name":"Ferreira, Manuel","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Fintan","key":"mcevoypau","last":"McEvoy","locid":"38cc3ea7-06d4-43f9-b239-092f41bcd16b","name":"McEvoy, Fintan","orcid":"0000-0003-4195-8816","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Mário","key":"ginjapau","last":"Ginja","name":"Ginja, Mário","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37215470"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3389/fvets.2023.1160200","issn":["22971769"],"journal":"Frontiers in Veterinary Science","paperid":"1160200","status":"p","title":"Frontiers in Veterinary Science","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85159923783"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-e712921a-d753-49b6-8bef-95587e27fef9"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Femoral parallelism : evaluation and impact of variation on canine hip dysplasia assessment","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-679349","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Significant coronary artery disease (CAD) is a frequent finding in patients with severe aortic stenosis undergoing transcatheter aortic valve implantation (TAVI), and the management of these two conditions becomes of particular importance with the extension of the procedure to younger and lower-risk patients. Yet, the preprocedural diagnostic evaluation and the indications for treatment of significant CAD in TAVI candidates remain a matter of debate. In this clinical consensus statement, a group of experts from the European Association of Percutaneous Cardiovascular Interventions (EAPCI) in collaboration with the European Society of Cardiology (ESC) Working Group on Cardiovascular Surgery aims to review the available evidence on the topic and proposes a rationale for the diagnostic evaluation and indications for percutaneous revascularisation of CAD in patients with severe aortic stenosis undergoing transcatheter treatment. Moreover, it also focuses on commissural alignment of transcatheter heart valves and coronary re-access after TAVI and redo-TAVI.","author_count":25,"contributors":["Tarantini, Giuseppe","Tang, Gilbert","Nai Fovino, Luca","Blackman, Daniel","Van Mieghem, Nicolas M.","Kim, Won Keun","Karam, Nicole","Carrilho-Ferreira, Pedro","Fournier, Stephane","Pręgowski, Jerzy","Fraccaro, Chiara","Vincent, Flavien","Campante Teles, Rui","Mylotte, Darren","Wong, Ivan","Bieliauskas, Gintautas","Czerny, Martin","Bonaros, Nikolaos","Parolari, Alessandro","Dudek, Darius","Tchetche, Didier","Eltchaninoff, Hélène","De Backer, Ole","Stefanini, Giulio","Søndergaard, Lars"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-690263"],"filters":{"added":"2023-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Austria","France","Germany","Ireland","Italy","Netherlands","Poland","Portugal","Switzerland","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","CHU Hôpitau