,"Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Niels Bohr Institute, Theoretical high energy, astroparticle and gravitational physics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Dipankar","key":"daspau","last":"Das","name":"Das, Dipankar","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"P. M.","key":"ferreirapau","last":"Ferreira","name":"Ferreira, P. M.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Antonio P.","key":"moraispau","last":"Morais","name":"Morais, Antonio P.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Ian","key":"padillagaypau","last":"Padilla-Gay","locid":"480e794b-5268-4d18-9e77-97440dd6432f","name":"Padilla-Gay, Ian","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Roman","key":"pasechnikpau","last":"Pasechnik","name":"Pasechnik, Roman","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"J. Pedro","key":"rodriguespau","last":"Rodrigues","name":"Rodrigues, J. Pedro","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/jhep11(2021)079","issn":["10298479","11266708"],"issn_org":["10298479"],"issue":"11","journal":"Journal of High Energy Physics","paperid":"079","status":"p","title":"Journal of High Energy Physics","volume":"2021"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85118967545"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-8f55ad63-01d6-4e08-bfe9-4ce0e5a0e336"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A three Higgs doublet model with symmetry-suppressed flavour changing neutral currents","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-479117","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"In this article, we suggest approaching writing as a vulnerable practice marked by an unstable boundary between bodies: bodies of text and bodies of writers. We present an exercise-method that we refer to as Monster Writing, which we have developed in order to engage with these instabilities as well as in order to address experiences of difficulty, anxiety and uncertainty in relation with the text and writing process. Though the writing process can at times be exciting and thrilling, and at other times perhaps a little tedious and mundane, for some it also presents (more than) occasional encounters with one's own insecurities, shame and doubt. We argue that this potentially more painful relationship between writer and text should be awarded more attention in scholarship on writing, and that a way of doing so is through the framework of feminist theory on vulnerability, embodiment, and the monstrous.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Henriksen, Line","Kjær, Katrine Meldgaard","Blønd, Marie","Cohn, Marisa","Cakici, Baki","Douglas-Jones, Rachel","Ferreira, Pedro","Feshak, Viktoriya","Gahoonia, Simy Kaur","Sandbukt, Sunniva"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383081"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Technical University of Munich"],"keyword":["Feminist theory","Monster theory","Organizational studies","Vulnerability","Writing"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"writing bodies and bodies of text thinking vulnerability through monsters","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/gwao.12782","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383081","identifier":[{"id":"85121653378","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-7b7818dc-cf90-442f-826c-fe4264251f64"],"keyword":["feminist theory","monster theory","organizational studies","vulnerability","writing"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-5993E6636350380725B0F7E6033835DC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/gwao.12782","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/gwao.12782","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"technical university of munich","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technical University of Munich","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Line","key":"henriksenpau","last":"Henriksen","name":"Henriksen, Line","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Katrine Meldgaard","key":"kjærpau","last":"Kjær","locid":"0c46864b-a7f8-4439-aeda-0781f77bfe88","name":"Kjær, Katrine Meldgaard","orcid":"0000-0001-7994-5186","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Marie","key":"bløndpau","last":"Blønd","name":"Blønd, Marie","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Baki","key":"cakicipau","last":"Cakici","locid":"b614ac6f-eefa-44a3-8b9a-092b88d86d37","name":"Cakici, Baki","orcid":"0000-0002-1039-8177","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rachel","key":"douglasjonespau","last":"Douglas-Jones","locid":"f11b0cc1-ad9f-4b12-b805-65b70e5bcd65","name":"Douglas-Jones, Rachel","orcid":"0000-0001-9562-6572","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pedro","key":"ferreirapau","last":"Ferreira","locid":"1b3cff7f-1549-4998-b52e-0ba9093e1d01","name":"Ferreira, Pedro","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Viktoriya","key":"feshakpau","last":"Feshak","name":"Feshak, Viktoriya","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Simy Kaur","key":"gahooniapau","last":"Gahoonia","locid":"6c543b1d-8137-489d-b444-d17921fc9de9","name":"Gahoonia, Simy Kaur","orcid":"0000-0001-9668-6868","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sunniva","key":"sandbuktpau","last":"Sandbukt","locid":"06d038a4-f659-4c53-8a6d-58e295ab603f","name":"Sandbukt, Sunniva","orcid":"0009-0002-4274-4991","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/gwao.12782","issn":["09686673","14680432"],"issn_org":["09686673"],"journal":"Gender, Work and Organization","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell","status":"p","title":"Gender, Work and Organization"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85121653378"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-7b7818dc-cf90-442f-826c-fe4264251f64"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Writing bodies and bodies of text: Thinking vulnerability through monsters","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383081","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Importance: Sodium glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitors reduce morbidity and mortality in patients with heart failure and reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF). Clinicians may find estimates of the projected long-term benefits of sodium glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitors a helpful addition to clinical trial results when communicating the benefits of this class of drug to patients. Objective: To estimate the projected long-term treatment effects of dapagliflozin in patients with HFrEF over the duration of a patient's lifetime. Design, Setting, and Participants: Exploratory analysis was performed of Dapagliflozin and Prevention of Adverse Outcomes in Heart Failure (DAPA-HF), a phase 3 randomized, placebo-controlled clinical trial conducted at 410 sites in 20 countries. Patients with an ejection fraction less than or equal to 40% in New York Heart Association functional classification II to IV and elevated plasma levels of N-terminal pro B-type natriuretic peptide were enrolled between February 15, 2017, and August 17, 2018, with final follow-up on June 6, 2019. Mean (SD) duration of follow-up was 17.6 (5.2) months. Interventions: Dapagliflozin, 10 mg, once daily vs placebo in addition to standard therapy. Main Outcomes and Measures: The primary composite outcome was time to first hospitalization for heart failure, urgent heart failure visit requiring intravenous therapy, or cardiovascular death. The trial results were extrapolated to estimate the projected long-term treatment effects of dapagliflozin over the duration of a patient's lifetime for the primary outcome and the secondary outcome of death from any cause. Results: A total of 4744 patients (1109 women [23.4%]; 3635 men [76.6%]) were randomized in DAPA-HF, with a mean (SD) age of 66.3 (10.9) years. The extrapolated mean event-free survival for an individual aged 65 years from a primary composite end point event was 6.2 years for placebo and 8.3 years for dapagliflozin, representing an event-free survival time gain of 2.1 years (95% CI, 0.8-3.3 years; P =.002). When considering death from any cause, mean extrapolated life expectancy for an individual aged 65 years was 9.1 years for placebo and 10.8 years for dapagliflozin, with a gain in survival of 1.7 years (95% CI, 0.1-3.3; P =.03) with dapagliflozin. Similar results were seen when extrapolated across the age range studied. In analyses of subgroups of patients in DAPA-HF, consistent benefits were seen with dapagliflozin on both event-free and overall survival. Conclusions and Relevance: These findings indicate that dapagliflozin provides clinically meaningful gains in extrapolated event-free and overall survival. These findings may be helpful in communicating the benefits of this treatment to patients with HFrEF. Trial Registration: ClinicalTrials.gov Identifier: NCT03036124.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Docherty, Kieran F.","Jhund, Pardeep S.","Claggett, Brian","Ferreira, João Pedro","Bengtsson, Olof","Inzucchi, Silvio E.","Køber, Lars","Kosiborod, Mikhail N.","Langkilde, Anna Maria","Martinez, Felipe A.","Ponikowski, Piotr","Sabatine, Marc S.","Sjöstrand, Mikaela","Solomon, Scott D.","McMurray, John J.V."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-441364"],"filters":{"added":"2022-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","America, South","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Argentina","Australia","France","Poland","Sweden","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","AstraZeneca, Sweden","Harvard University","Institut National de la Santé et de la Recherche Médicale (INSERM)","University of Cordoba","University of Glasgow","University of Missouri at Kansas City","University of New South Wales","Wrocław Medical University","Yale University"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"extrapolating long term event free and overall survival with dapagliflozin in patients with heart failure and reduced ejection fraction an exploratory analysis of a phase 000000000003 randomized clinical trial","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-441364","identifier":[{"id":"34319398","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC8576587","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85111962406","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-50402f4b-6c87-4e15-a1c5-54e5bd31c667"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A9F53A462425FEA74D1ACB386045EE5D"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"university of glasgow","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Glasgow","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"harvard university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Harvard University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"fr","key":"institut national de la santé et de la recherche médicale","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Institut National de la Santé et de la Recherche Médicale (INSERM)","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"se","key":"astrazeneca sweden","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AstraZeneca, Sweden","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"us","key":"yale university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Yale University","used":1},{"affno":"6","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Internal medicine: Cardiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"7","country":"us","key":"university of missouri at kansas city","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Missouri at Kansas City","used":1},{"affno":"8","country":"au","key":"university of new south wales","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"University of New South Wales","used":1},{"affno":"9","country":"ar","key":"university of cordoba","loc":["Argentina","America, South"],"name":"University of Cordoba","used":1},{"affno":"10","country":"pl","key":"wrocław medical university","loc":["Poland","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Wrocław Medical University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Kieran F.","key":"dochertypau","last":"Docherty","name":"Docherty, Kieran F.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Pardeep S.","key":"jhundpau","last":"Jhund","name":"Jhund, Pardeep S.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Brian","key":"claggettpau","last":"Claggett","name":"Claggett, Brian","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","first":"João Pedro","key":"ferreirapau","last":"Ferreira","name":"Ferreira, João Pedro","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Olof","key":"bengtssonpau","last":"Bengtsson","name":"Bengtsson, Olof","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","first":"Silvio E.","key":"inzucchipau","last":"Inzucchi","name":"Inzucchi, Silvio E.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"6","first":"Lars","key":"køberpau","last":"Køber","locid":"e3f0c57c-605a-431a-9c63-2213c46621e6","name":"Køber, Lars","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"7 8","first":"Mikhail N.","key":"kosiborodpau","last":"Kosiborod","name":"Kosiborod, Mikhail N.","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Anna Maria","key":"langkildepau","last":"Langkilde","name":"Langkilde, Anna Maria","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"9","first":"Felipe A.","key":"martinezpau","last":"Martinez","name":"Martinez, Felipe A.","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"10","first":"Piotr","key":"ponikowskipau","last":"Ponikowski","name":"Ponikowski, Piotr","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Marc S.","key":"sabatinepau","last":"Sabatine","name":"Sabatine, Marc S.","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Mikaela","key":"sjöstrandpau","last":"Sjöstrand","name":"Sjöstrand, Mikaela","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Scott D.","key":"solomonpau","last":"Solomon","name":"Solomon, Scott D.","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"John J.V.","key":"mcmurraypau","last":"McMurray","name":"McMurray, John J.V.","rank":15,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34319398"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1001/jamacardio.2021.2632","issn":["23806583","23806591"],"issn_org":["23806583"],"issue":"11","journal":"JAMA Cardiology","pages":"1298-1305","status":"p","title":"JAMA Cardiology","volume":"6"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85111962406"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-50402f4b-6c87-4e15-a1c5-54e5bd31c667"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Extrapolating Long-term Event-Free and Overall Survival with Dapagliflozin in Patients with Heart Failure and Reduced Ejection Fraction : An Exploratory Analysis of a Phase 3 Randomized Clinical Trial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-441364","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"White matter (WM) abnormalities in patients with cocaine use disorder (CUD) have been studied; however, the reported effects on the human brain are heterogenous and most results have been obtained from male participants. In addition, biological data supporting the imaging findings and revealing possible mechanisms underlying the neurotoxic effects of chronic cocaine use (CU) on WM are largely restricted to animal studies. To evaluate the neurotoxic effects of CU in the WM, we performed an in vivo diffusion tensor imaging assessment of male and female cocaine users (n = 75) and healthy controls (HC) (n = 58). Moreover, we performed an ex vivo large-scale proteomic analysis using liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry in postmortem brains of patients with CUD (n = 8) and HC (n = 12). Compared with the HC, the CUD group showed significant reductions in global fractional anisotropy (FA) (p < 0.001), and an increase in global mean (MD) and radial diffusion (RD) (both p < 0.001). The results revealed that FA, RD, and MD alterations in the CUD group were widespread along the major WM tracts, after analysis using the tract-based special statistics approach. Global FA was negatively associated with years of CU (p = 0.0421) and female sex (p < 0.001), but not with years of alcohol or nicotine use. Concerning the fibers connecting the left to the right prefrontal cortex, Brodmann area 9 (BA9), the CUD group presented lower FA (p = 0.006) and higher RD (p < 0.001) values compared with the HC group. A negative association between the duration of CU in life and FA values in this tract was also observed (p = 0.019). Proteomics analyses in BA9 found 11 proteins differentially expressed between cocaine users and controls. Among these, were proteins related to myelination and neuroinflammation. In summary, we demonstrate convergent evidence from in vivo diffusion tensor imaging and ex vivo proteomics analysis of WM disruption in CUD.","author_count":16,"contributors":["Tondo, Lucca Pizzato","Viola, Thiago Wendt","Fries, Gabriel R.","Kluwe-Schiavon, Bruno","Rothmann, Leonardo Mello","Cupertino, Renata","Ferreira, Pedro","Franco, Alexandre Rosa","Lane, Scott D.","Stertz, Laura","Zhao, Zhongming","Hu, Ruifeng","Meyer, Thomas","Schmitz, Joy M.","Walss-Bass, Consuelo","Grassi-Oliveira, Rodrigo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-132277"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","America, South","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Grassi-Oliveira, Rodrigo"],"country":["Brazil","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Nathan S. Kline Institute for Psychiatric Research","Pontificia Universidade Catolica do Rio Grande do Sul","University of Texas","University of Vermont"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"white matter deficits in cocaine use disorder convergent evidence from in vivo diffusion tensor imaging and ex vivo proteomic analysis","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41398-021-01367-x","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-132277","identifier":[{"id":"PMC8081729","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"33911068","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85105102477","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-05360960-633f-49e6-b72a-27aafc0b81d8"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-7743F6BB5C8DDB6AC81C2249E67AD3FB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41398-021-01367-x","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"br","key":"pontificia universidade catolica do rio grande do sul","loc":["Brazil","America, South"],"name":"Pontificia Universidade Catolica do Rio Grande do Sul","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"university of texas","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Texas","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"university of vermont","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Vermont","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"us","key":"nathan s kline institute for psychiatric research","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Nathan S. Kline Institute for Psychiatric Research","used":1},{"affno":"5","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Translational Neuropsychiatry Unit"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lucca Pizzato","key":"tondopau","last":"Tondo","name":"Tondo, Lucca Pizzato","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thiago Wendt","key":"violapau","last":"Viola","name":"Viola, Thiago Wendt","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Gabriel R.","key":"friespau","last":"Fries","name":"Fries, Gabriel R.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bruno","key":"kluweschiavonpau","last":"Kluwe-Schiavon","name":"Kluwe-Schiavon, Bruno","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leonardo Mello","key":"rothmannpau","last":"Rothmann","name":"Rothmann, Leonardo Mello","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Renata","key":"cupertinopau","last":"Cupertino","name":"Cupertino, Renata","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pedro","key":"ferreirapau","last":"Ferreira","name":"Ferreira, Pedro","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Alexandre Rosa","key":"francopau","last":"Franco","name":"Franco, Alexandre Rosa","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Scott D.","key":"lanepau","last":"Lane","name":"Lane, Scott D.","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Laura","key":"stertzpau","last":"Stertz","name":"Stertz, Laura","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Zhongming","key":"zhaopau","last":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhongming","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ruifeng","key":"hupau","last":"Hu","name":"Hu, Ruifeng","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"meyerpau","last":"Meyer","name":"Meyer, Thomas","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Joy M.","key":"schmitzpau","last":"Schmitz","name":"Schmitz, Joy M.","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Consuelo","key":"walssbasspau","last":"Walss-Bass","name":"Walss-Bass, Consuelo","rank":15,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 5","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rodrigo","key":"grassioliveirapau","last":"Grassi-Oliveira","locid":"fa6d6df6-793a-4406-96e1-cd40f0604227","name":"Grassi-Oliveira, Rodrigo","orcid":"0000-0001-9911-5921","rank":16,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["33911068"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41398-021-01367-x","issn":["21583188"],"issue":"1","journal":"Translational Psychiatry","paperid":"252","publisher":"Springer Nature","status":"p","title":"Translational Psychiatry","volume":"11"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85105102477"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-05360960-633f-49e6-b72a-27aafc0b81d8"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"White matter deficits in cocaine use disorder : convergent evidence from in vivo diffusion tensor imaging and ex vivo proteomic analysis","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-132277","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Today many platforms host user generated content (UGC) and content by professionals side-by-side. However, their behavior is mostly studied in isolation. In this paper we study the spillover effects from UCG on content from professionals in the context of the news industry. We use an exogenous shock to the supply of user generated content to study spillover effects on the output of professionals as well as the implications for the business model of online platforms. Using data from an online news platform, we find that in response to the reduction in supply of user generated news, which mainly is driven by reduced output from experienced users, inexperienced professionals significantly increase their output. However, we find a decrease in overall content on the platform which is especially pronounced for local news. We further show that this likely has detrimental effects on the business model of the online platform with a decrease in viewership and articles as well as an increase in the number of paid professionals. Thus, our work contributes to the literature of user generated contentand online platforms as well as the literature on local news.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Claussen, Jörg","Ferreira, Pedro","Grad, Tom","Sen, Ananya"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-281215"],"event":{"acronym":"AOM Annual Meeting","end":"2021-08-04","place":"Virtual, Online","start":"2021-07-29","title":"The Academy of Management Annual Meeting 2021","year":"2021"},"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Carnegie Mellon University"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"local news ugc and professionals evidence from citizen journalism","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-281215","ids":["cbs-3d3f7a29-9374-4c27-b7f0-68b01c7a9adf"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-F0BFD46D347982B8B5C447F4EFB797EE"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Strategy and Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"carnegie mellon university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Carnegie Mellon University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jörg","key":"claussenpau","last":"Claussen","locid":"28989f02-fc99-463e-a60a-c857199c7771","name":"Claussen, Jörg","orcid":"0000-0001-8432-8860","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Pedro","key":"ferreirapau","last":"Ferreira","name":"Ferreira, Pedro","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Tom","key":"gradpau","last":"Grad","locid":"dbb18f05-026e-4604-9cbf-7a1e150d27c5","name":"Grad, Tom","orcid":"0000-0003-0528-173X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ananya","key":"senpau","last":"Sen","name":"Sen, Ananya","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Sonia","key":"tanejapedh","last":"Taneja","name":"Taneja, Sonia","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.5465/ambpp.2021.12646abstract","issn":["21516561"],"place":"Briarcliff Manor, NY","publisher":"Academy of Management","series":"Academy of Management Proceedings","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the Eighty-first Annual Meeting of the Academy of Management"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-3d3f7a29-9374-4c27-b7f0-68b01c7a9adf"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Local News, UGC and Professionals : Evidence from Citizen Journalism","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-281215","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Background: Many weight loss programs show short-term effectiveness, but subsequent weight loss maintenance is difficult to achieve. Digital technologies offer a promising means of delivering behavior change approaches at low costs and on a wide scale. The Navigating to a Healthy Weight (NoHoW) project, which was funded by the European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation program, aimed to develop, test, and evaluate a digital toolkit designed to promote successful long-term weight management. The toolkit was tested in an 18-month, large-scale, international, 2×2 factorial (motivation and self-regulation vs emotion regulation) randomized controlled trial that was conducted on adults with overweight or obesity who lost ≥5% of their body weight in the preceding 12 months before enrollment into the intervention. Objective: This paper aims to describe the development of the NoHoW Toolkit, focusing on the logic models, content, and specifications, as well as the results from user testing. Methods: The toolkit was developed by using a systematic approach, which included the development of the theory-based logic models, the selection of behavior change techniques, the translation of these techniques into a web-based app (NoHoW Toolkit components), technical development, and the user evaluation and refinement of the toolkit. Results: The toolkit included a set of web-based tools and inputs from digital tracking devices (smart scales and activity trackers) and modules that targeted weight, physical activity, and dietary behaviors. The final toolkit comprised 34 sessions that were distributed through 15 modules and provided active content over a 4-month period. The motivation and self-regulation arm consisted of 8 modules (17 sessions), the emotion regulation arm was presented with 7 modules (17 sessions), and the combined arm received the full toolkit (15 modules; 34 sessions). The sessions included a range of implementations, such as videos, testimonies, and questionnaires. Furthermore, the toolkit contained 5 specific data tiles for monitoring weight, steps, healthy eating, mood, and sleep. Conclusions: A systematic approach to the development of digital solutions based on theory, evidence, and user testing may significantly contribute to the advancement of the science of behavior change and improve current solutions for sustained weight management. Testing the toolkit by using a 2×2 design provided a unique opportunity to examine the effect of motivation and self-regulation and emotion regulation separately, as well as the effect of their interaction in weight loss maintenance.","author_count":20,"contributors":["Marques, Marta M.","Matos, Marcela","Mattila, Elina","Encantado, Jorge","Duarte, Cristiana","Teixeira, Pedro J.","Stubbs, R. James","Sniehotta, Falko F.","Ermes, Miikka","Harjumaa, Marja","Leppänen, Juha","Välkkynen, Pasi","Silva, Marlene N.","Ferreira, Cláudia","Carvalho, Sérgio","Palmeira, Lara","Horgan, Graham","Heitmann, Berit Lilienthal","Evans, Elizabeth H.","Palmeira, António L."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-493326"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Finland","Netherlands","Portugal","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Durham University","Lusófona University","NOVA University Lisbon","The James Hutton Institute","University of Coimbra","University of Lisbon","University of Newcastle","University of Twente","VTT Technical Research Centre of Finland Ltd.","York St John University"],"keyword":["Behavior change techniques","Emotion regulation","Logic models","Motivation","Self monitoring","Self regulation","User testing","Weight management","mHealth"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":[